{
  "id": "Compound::DB00959 - Disease::DOID:9074",
  "verbalization": "Methylprednisolone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) through a mechanism involving the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid pathway. This pathway is shared by Methylprednisolone and Hydrocortisone, both of which are corticosteroids that act on the corticosteroid hormone receptor. The evidence suggests that Methylprednisolone may exert a therapeutic effect on SLE by modulating the immune system, potentially through the activation of the corticosteroid receptor, which is known to suppress inflammatory responses. The presence of a common ancestor in the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid pathway further supports a shared mechanism of action between Methylprednisolone and Hydrocortisone in influencing SLE pathology. While the direct causal link between Methylprednisolone and SLE remains indirect, the integration of its role in the corticosteroid receptor pathway and its association with SLE through shared biochemical pathways provides a plausible explanation for its potential use in treating this condition.",
